[Music]  this factory in germany has made engines  for some of the most popular cars in the  world for over five decades  ever wish you owned a volkswagen but  more recently  some of those cars have lost their shine  vw owners coming to grips with the  realization that their diesel cars can  emit from 10  to 40 times more pollutants we all know  that  we have let down customers what's more  global sales of internal combustion  engines are predicted to drop by over 50  by 2035. the hot new market electric  vehicles  so to try and capture that market vw is  transforming this engine factory  into its new ev battery facility the  first of six that it plans to build in  europe  at a cost of about 15 billion dollars  according to analysts  what you see behind has nothing to do  with combustion engines and that shows  the size of this transformation that  means going from making combustion  engines that have more than 2  000 moving parts to high-tech electric  motors that only have about 20  and changing its workforce to do that  traditional automakers  if they want to be in this market in  2035 they need to develop just as much  as the startups need to develop the ev  market is currently dominated by tesla  and faces disruption from a bunch of new  startups they have nothing to lose they  start from scratch  we have a lot to lose so could this  factory be a blueprint for traditional  automakers to survive the ev revolution  [Music]  while vw says that its battery factory  and salt skitter  won't be online for another two years or  so tesla already has  several gigafactories in operation and  completed its shanghai facility  in under a year it also says it's  nearing completion of a huge plant here  in germany  we've decided to put tesla gigafactory  europe  uh in the berlin area other well-funded  startups like lucid and rivien have set  up brand new factories to start ev  production this year  so legacy automakers like gm ford and vw  have all announced they're investing  billions into ev production to defend  their market share  with vw committing to 43 billion dollars  in five years  when you look at what the startups are  doing they don't have to transition you  don't have to change a legacy  a legacy footprints so traditional  automakers don't have  the luxury of sitting back and saying ah  we'll wait and see how it goes  we are responsible for the people and  for our customers  and we have to transform so how is vw  investing that money in saltskitter  well some of those decisions are made by  this engineer frank blohm  we are building a small-scale cells  right now and now we are building up the  first mass production plant  vw has converted part of its existing  factory into labs and a pilot line  to test and refine the science behind  its batteries it plans to build a brand  new facility right next to it which  could produce batteries for over 600 000  vehicles a year  and is expected to cost around 2.5  billion dollars according to analysts  although it says it hasn't finalized the  designs for its new facility yet  the factory is expected to be similar to  this one in sweden it's owned by battery  supplier north vault  and vw is a major investor in the  company  thomas schmoll is ceo of vw's component  division  the bottleneck will be how fast you can  establish  increase and ramp up your battery  business within its labs vw is trying to  figure out what kind of batteries its  electric  vehicles will need in the future it's  testing battery recycling technology  and to get ahead of the competition it's  experimenting with next generation  solid state batteries which don't  contain liquid lithium  and promise far faster charging and  greater mileage fast charge is a big big  topic for us we are working on that  with with the big workforce the  transition will affect everyone in this  plant  the company will need fewer mechanical  engineers and will need new people with  totally different skills like  electrochemists or developers  as evs needs so much software that vw is  set to become one of the largest tech  companies in europe  to find the right people is a challenge  the new industry everybody wants  electrochemistry people  i think the big outstanding question is  we we know that it will take  fewer employees to build  a battery cell pack so therefore how do  you figure that out  where do you transition people to as the  battery plant grows over time  part of the staff will be retrained  while some legacy jobs are expected to  go away  at the end of this transition it's  unclear whether the overall number of  workers on this site will go down  globally vw has already made some cuts  in 2019 the company announced that it  was cutting some  7 000 jobs over 5 years and since 2016  it has eliminated at least 23 000  positions  by offering early retirement and not  replacing workers  we always have this this um issue oh  maybe it's destroying business no it's  not right you can if you do it right you  can  create jobs we will have 1 500 1 600  employees in uh in the new cell business  what we are doing here and that's not  um the end because this is only the  first portion the first part of the cell  plant  the stakes are particularly high for vw  as it's trying to change its image as a  polluter  while also meeting new environmental  regulations around the world  after the emissions scandal in 2015 vw  pleaded guilty to installing a device on  its vehicles to cheat us emissions tests  it is not only our cars we have to fix  we know we have to repair our  credibility  too today as vw bets big on electric  its u.s sales are much lower than they  were in 2012.  i think it does dovetail with with  needing to prove that they're clean and  and having that kind of hanging over  their heads  um because it does the news doesn't stop  it still still pop up so the issue isn't  completely dead  so just like this factory the  decades-old company has to reinvent  itself to keep up with technology  and beat competitors old and new

